From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: PHIL: But what if _everyone_ did X? (was: Is the State a "person"?)

Dave Krieger writes:
>[1] There is some familiar rhetorical term for the fallacious use of the
>"But what if _everyone_ did X?" argument that is right on the tip of my
>tongue.

I don't know the phrase, but there is an obvious fallacy in this and the
Kantian categorical imperative: how one describes the action in question
(or in Kant's terms, by what principle one considers oneself to be acting)
affects the meaning of "everyone doing it".

The descriptions "getting free postage on all my letters", "getting free
postage when I can't afford the cost of a stamp", and "attacking statist
monopolies" may all apply to the same action, yet universal adoption of
each of these would have very different consequences.  For another example,
compare "persecuting degenerate scum" and "persecuting anyone I don't
like".

Craig Presson writes:
>I'm sure Tim Starr will be Johnny-on-the-spot with the whole history
>of the Kantian imperative and the Randian criticism thereof; thus we
>benefit from having a philosopher on the list (in case you were
>wondering :-).

I suspect the Randian criticism, if there is one, will be rather different
from mine, resting as mine does on the denial of a unique principle or
fundamental nature underlying an action.

