Subject: SPACE: DC-X Flies

I've been off the net for a few weeks to actually work on some of these
launch concepts.  Things like railguns tend to have large initial costs.
Consider a railroad track.  You have two steel rails, wooden ties, and
gravel ballast, all cheap materials.  With a railgun, you need the same
rails (now to conduct a high current), but you need a big coil or rotating
energy store, or a lot of batteries.  The energy storage tends to have
expensive parts, so driving up the cost of the launcher.  The air force
has worked on railguns, but they haven't been scaled up to large size
yet.  The biggest one I know about used 10,000 Sears Die-hard Batteries
in parallel to generate the multi-million Amp current to fire the railgun.
It fired a projectile in the 1 pound range.

Being on the equator gives you 460 m/s from the rotation of the Earth.
Not being on the equator you get cos(Latitude)x460 m/s.  So you don't
lose too much if you are reasonably close to the equator.  Air drag
losses are a function of how big your vehicle is, but typically you lose 
a couple of hundred m/s to drag during a launch.  If you start at
higher altitude, you avoid some of this.

Scramjets are a new type of flying vehicle propulsion.  New propulsion
engines take on the order of a billion dollars to develop.  Since I am
trying to come up with a launch system that in total costs less than
several hundred million to develop, and preferably under 100 million,
I can't afford a new engine.  Someday, when launch traffic is large
enough, you can use new engines, but to get started you have to work with
what is there, unless you are a government and can afford to spend
a few billion.

Dani Eder

